{#
  The polling wrapper around the trial queue table — the mirror of
  _patient_rows_frame.html.twig.

  Rendered in BOTH places that show the table: inline from new_by_patient.html.twig
  on first load, and on its own from the rows endpoint on every poll. Same
  template both times, so the server-rendered markup and the refreshed markup
  cannot drift apart.

  Expects everything _trial_rows.html.twig expects, plus `patient`.

  ── Same frame id as the other flow, on purpose ──────────────────────────────
  Turbo matches a response's frame to the one on the page BY ID, and only one of
  the two flows is ever on screen at a time. Reusing `evaluation-rows` means the
  evaluation-poll Stimulus controller works here untouched — it reloads whatever
  frame it is attached to and reads data-active from inside it, neither of which
  cares which axis the table is showing. A second id would have bought nothing
  and required the controller to learn about flows.

  target="_top" for the same reason it is set on the other frame: without it,
  every link and form in here would navigate *within* the frame, so the Queue
  form's redirect would swallow its flash message (those live outside the frame)
  and "View Result" would try to render a whole detail page inside a table cell.
  Only the poller's programmatic reload() updates the frame.
#}
<turbo-frame id="evaluation-rows"
             target="_top"
             data-controller="evaluation-poll"
             {# `match` travels with the poll URL so a refresh re-asks the SAME
                question. Without it the table would quietly repopulate from a
                different filter on the next tick, with nothing on screen to
                explain why the row count changed. #}
             data-evaluation-poll-url-value="{{ path('lumina_ui_evaluation_new_by_patient_rows', {patient: patient.personId, match: matchFilter.value}) }}"
             data-evaluation-poll-interval-value="5000">
  {% include '@LuminaUi/evaluation/_trial_rows.html.twig' %}
</turbo-frame>
